From: Ronnie Overacker <ronnie_overacker@cuesta.edu>
Sent: Friday, April 24, 2026 7:57 PM
To: Kyle Nessen <kyle_nessen@cuesta.edu>; Lexie Bell <lexie_bell@cuesta.edu=
>; Aubrey Priest <aubrey_priest@cuesta.edu>
Subject: Draft UAS Program Proposal

Hi Aubrey, Lexie, and Kyle,
Attached is the draft UAS Technology program proposal. This is still very m=
uch a working draft, but I wanted to get it in front of you so you could ta=
ke a look before I keep refining it.
The section I=92d especially like you to look at is the equipment/software =
and course development cost estimate. The numbers are higher than what we o=
riginally talked about, a year or so ago, and I think there are two main re=
asons for that.
First, the NDAA / American Security Drone Act issue is pushing us toward ND=
AA-compliant platforms for future purchases. I realize SWP is state funding=
 and not the same as Perkins, but the industry is clearly moving this direc=
tion, especially in public safety, utilities, government, and infrastructur=
e inspection. My concern is that if we build this around cheaper non-compli=
ant platforms, we may save money up front but end up training students on e=
quipment many employers are already moving away from.
Second, we are now adding ag spray operations, and we also need payload-cap=
able equipment that can support multispectral work. That is a different lev=
el of equipment than basic flight, mapping, or inspection, and it pushes th=
e estimate up. At the same time, it gives the agriculture pathway more subs=
tance and makes the program more relevant to the Central Coast.
I tried to frame the cost section as phased and estimated, not as something=
 we would need to buy all at once. Year 1 uses the DJI 4T and 4E we already=
 have and focuses on the core launch courses. The higher costs are tied to =
later phases if we move forward with agriculture, public safety, and other =
specialized pathways.
I also don=92t know how to properly fill out the funding sources section ye=
t, or whether this is even feasible with SWP funding at the level described=
. I added the Foundation as a possible source in hopes we might be able to =
pursue some industry support, either through equipment donations or in-kind=
 contributions, but I don=92t know if that is realistic yet.
Any feedback on whether the cost ranges feel reasonable, whether the phased=
 rollout is clear, and how we should frame the funding section would be ver=
y helpful.
I would like to submit this for initial approval to the College Council, so=
 we can start building coursework in CurriQNet over the summer.
The last College Council meeting of this semester is May 11th, so I would l=
ike to get this item on the agenda and this proposal submitted.
Thanks,
Ron


--_000_DM6PR02MB62836584DB45CC5ABC2EC3608B282DM6PR02MB6283namp_
